WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.130
Imagine team meetings that feel genuinely warm and friendly. People

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.950
like each other. But if you watch closely, the same

3
00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.190
few people's ideas are the ones that get acted on,

4
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.070
and colleagues who work in the field rarely shape the

5
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.390
decisions that affect their work.

6
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.460
That is a subtle picture, because nothing in it looks

7
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.560
unkind. Everyone is welcome in the room. So what does

8
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.280
the page mean by inclusive leadership?

9
00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.770
It describes a deliberate, daily practice of building psychological safety,

10
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:31.170
belonging and fair influence, so people across every identity, background,

11
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.910
disability and point of view can contribute as themselves and

12
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:38.430
actually shape the results. The key word is influence. Being

13
00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:39.890
invited in is only the beginning.

14
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.960
That sounds close to democratic leadership, where everyone gets a

15
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.140
chance to speak. How is inclusive leadership different from that?

16
00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:53.050
Democratic leadership invites participation from whoever speaks up. Inclusive leadership

17
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.230
notices who is missing, who is quiet and whose ideas

18
00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.810
are being discounted, and changes how the group works so

19
00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.670
every voice carries weight. It also differs from transformative leadership,

20
00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:07.810
which works mainly on large structures. Inclusive leadership works where

21
00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:11.550
people experience belonging every day: the team and its relationships.

22
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.720
If I sat in on a meeting run by an

23
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.400
inclusive leader, what would I notice that I might miss

24
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.320
in a typical meeting?

25
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.690
Attention to airtime. When someone is interrupted, the leader brings

26
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.410
it back to them. When an idea gets repeated and

27
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.510
starts to sound like someone else's, the leader returns the

28
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:31.190
credit, with something like, I want to go back to

29
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.170
the point Maria raised earlier.

30
00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.710
Before the meeting, there is a clear agenda shared ahead

31
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.250
of time, captions, plain language and several ways to contribute,

32
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:44.250
so neurodivergent, disabled and multilingual colleagues take part fully without

33
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.630
having to ask each time.

34
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.700
And what about the leader's own attitude? I imagine it

35
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.920
matters a great deal whether people feel safe to disagree

36
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:51.440
with them.

37
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.430
It does. The leader admits what they do not know,

38
00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:00.110
asks real questions about unfamiliar perspectives, and treats respectful disagreement

39
00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.730
as a sign of a healthy team. When someone makes

40
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.890
an exclusionary assumption, or a hiring panel leans on a

41
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.970
vague sense of culture fit, the leader calmly brings the

42
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.150
group back to clear criteria.

43
00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:16.050
Research from Deloitte names six traits: visible commitment, humility, awareness

44
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.690
of bias, curiosity, cultural intelligence and effective collaboration.

45
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.900
Why does this matter so much in public service, where

46
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.940
the work is about the people being served as much

47
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:26.880
as the people on the team?

48
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.950
Because habits travel. Teams that practice cultural humility and accessible

49
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.030
communication with each other carry those habits into conversations with

50
00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:39.350
residents, families and partners. Diverse teams also outperform others on

51
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.430
complex problems only when leadership is inclusive.

52
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:46.350
And it helps keep talented people: colleagues from underrepresented groups

53
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:50.810
often leave because daily team culture feels alienating, and inclusive

54
00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.490
leadership addresses exactly that.

55
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.240
Every style has a blind spot. Where does inclusive leadership

56
00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.740
tend to go wrong, even when the leader means well?

57
00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.550
The most common mistake is confusing inclusion with harmony. Inclusion

58
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.650
does not mean everyone agrees. It means people feel safe

59
00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.010
enough to disagree honestly. When a leader treats inclusion as

60
00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.550
keeping everyone happy, hard decisions stall. A second risk is

61
00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.510
mistaking warmth for fairness: a friendly team cannot, on its

62
00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.570
own, fix unequal pay, biased promotion criteria or unequal service.

63
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.600
The page names a risk that can happen with the

64
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.440
best intentions, and one it calls the greatest threat of

65
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.120
all. Can you describe both?

66
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.310
The first is leaning on colleagues from underrepresented groups to

67
00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.550
educate everyone, turning to the same person again and again

68
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:39.130
for the disability perspective or the community perspective. An inclusive

69
00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.830
leader does their own homework and lets each colleague be

70
00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:42.390
a whole professional.

71
00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.630
The greatest threat is performative tokenism: inviting diverse staff to

72
00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:51.730
listening sessions and committees, then ignoring their recommendations. As the

73
00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:55.470
page says, inclusion without real influence erodes trust faster than

74
00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:55.630
silence.

75
00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.620
So back to those warm, friendly meetings. Welcoming field colleagues

76
00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.660
more warmly seems kind, but it would not change who

77
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.380
shapes the decisions.

78
00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:10.550
Exactly. A stronger response changes how decisions are made: rotate

79
00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.490
who sets the agenda, gather field input before meetings, and

80
00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:17.790
show afterward how that input shaped the outcome. Inclusion means

81
00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.690
influence, and changing the structure lets everyone shape the results.

82
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.420
What else does the page suggest for anyone who wants

83
00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.520
to lead more inclusively, starting this week?

84
00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.650
Build inclusive habits into routine work so they happen even

85
00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:36.410
on busy days: accessible meeting practices, structured turns so everyone

86
00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.990
is heard, and clear criteria for hiring and promotion. Measure

87
00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:44.470
belonging alongside representation, so you know whether inclusion is actually

88
00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.170
felt in every group. And pair daily belonging with structural

89
00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:49.730
change and clear accountability.

90
00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.360
Here is a question to carry with you. On your

91
00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:56.280
team, who feels welcome, but has little say in the

92
00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.640
decisions that matter? Thank you for listening.
